-\section{Introduction}
-Agents that invoke tools can change operational state: they can update a record, publish a revised value, or commit a result that later actions treat as authoritative. The execution problem therefore extends beyond generating a plausible answer. A persistent change needs an accountable connection to the proposal and authorization that justified it. Human-in-the-loop frameworks already support pausing tool calls for approval, editing their arguments, and resuming execution \citep{langchainHITL}. These capabilities make the interval between review and later execution a concrete part of an agent workflow.
-
-Correction makes that interval especially consequential. A machine proposes a value $x_c$, and a reviewer authorizes $x_a$. A valid correction permits $x_c\jneq x_a$: if the machine reads 100 and the reviewer corrects it to 101, the authoritative state should contain 101 while preserving the original proposal. The proposal explains what the machine produced; the authorization explains why a different value may be committed. Guided repair and interactive cleaning already distinguish system suggestions from human judgments \citep{yakout2011guided,he2016falcon}. Executing the corrected action adds a further question: which proposal instance was reviewed, and which instance actually supplied the committed transition?
-
-Consider two persisted candidates $c_1$ and $c_2$. Both propose 100 for the same field, refer to the same evidence and predecessor, and retain the same producer and review context. They differ only in instance identity. The reviewer inspects $c_1$ and authorizes 101. Before committing, the agent regenerates an equivalent preview or receives a queued handoff carrying $c_2$. The final value may still be 101 and all contextual checks may agree. Yet review of $c_1$ does not authorize $c_2$ under an instance-accountability requirement. Equality of content and continuity of the reviewed instance answer different questions. A workflow must make that distinction explicit before deciding whether the continuation may mutate authoritative state.
-
-Several established mechanisms can implement parts of the required connection. Transactions install atomic state changes \citep{gray1981transaction}; concurrency checks bind them to a current predecessor \citep{kung1981occ}; provenance records derivation and responsibility \citep{buneman2006curated,moreau2013provdm}; approval and request binding control which actions proceed \citep{clark1987integrity,owaspTransactionAuthorization}. The question we examine is their joint correction case: how the machine proposal, authorized correction, reviewed candidate and executed transition remain distinguishable across persistence and subsequent agent actions. It has both a systems dimension---specifying and enforcing the relation---and an execution dimension---what the agent does when that relation produces rejection feedback.
-
-We make correction-aware review-to-execution continuity explicit, enforceable, and observable in state-changing agent workflows. Our central question is: \emph{How should authorization preserve review-to-execution continuity when corrected agent proposals become persistent changes, and how does enforcing that continuity reshape subsequent execution and recovery?} We compare retained-context and instance-bound authorization after the same prospective reviewed history, tracing feedback to the next meaningful agent action and eventual utility and integrity outcomes. This links the persistence contract to the environment in which an adaptive agent continues its task.
-
-The paper has four contributions:
-\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*,itemsep=2pt]
-\item \textbf{Correction-aware continuity.} We distinguish proposal value, authorized correction, reviewed instance and executed instance, and characterize the information needed to distinguish the declared continuity failures.
-\item \textbf{Executable authorization contract.} We connect the reviewed target to freshness, complete successors, evidence and field sources, with bounded formal checking, transactional realizations and independent persisted-state auditing.
-\item \textbf{Cross-layer mechanism evidence.} Controlled policy separation, request-binding tests and archived hosted-agent correction chains establish complementary evidence for the specified relation.
-\item \textbf{Prospective online intervention.} A frozen paired experiment inserts the policies into live agent continuations to measure recovery paths, separate utility/integrity outcomes and operational friction.
-\end{enumerate}
-Three findings organize the evidence. Correction creates a distinct continuity problem. The relation can be specified and enforced across persistence mechanisms. In standardized online handoffs, its enforcement redirects subsequent agent actions and adds measurable execution work. Seventeen context-admitted substitutions violate the instance-continuity oracle; the paired bound continuations instead complete with intact continuity. In the agent-managed preview tasks, neither policy produces an observed substitution. This contrast connects an execution-layer distinction to actual feedback and recovery, rather than presuming that agents ordinarily choose an invalid continuation.
